---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import GuideDoc from '@/components/GuideDoc.astro';
import { LINKS } from '@/data/site';
import { OTHER_PROJECTS } from '@/data/projects';

const playground = OTHER_PROJECTS.find((p) => p.name === 'contributor-playground')!;
---

<BaseLayout title="Why Open Source" description="Why you, as a college student, should care about open source.">
  <GuideDoc
    title="Why Should I Care About Open Source?"
    subtitle="Here we address exactly why you, as a college student, would want to care about Open Source."
  >
    <article class="doc__sec doc__prose reveal">
      <p class="muted">
        If you're a college student wondering whether open source is worth your time, the short
        answer is yes. Here are five reasons why, and how to get started.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h3">The industry runs on it</h2>
      <p class="muted">
        Open source isn't a side hobby next to "real" software work. It's how a lot of real
        software gets made. GitHub's 2017 Open Source Survey found that among employed
        developers:
      </p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li><span><strong>94%</strong> use open source at least sometimes in their work</span></li>
        <li><span><strong>81%</strong> use it frequently</span></li>
        <li><span><strong>65%</strong> of those who contribute back do it as part of their job</span></li>
      </ul>
      <div class="note">
        <span>
          Even Microsoft, a company that once called open source its biggest enemy, now says
          "Microsoft <span class="heart" aria-label="loves">&#9829;&#xFE0E;</span> Open Source".
        </span>
      </div>
      <p class="muted">
        When an employer sees open-source work on your profile, they see someone who already
        works the way their teams do.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h3">It's the best way to learn</h2>
      <p class="muted">
        Coursework hands you small, tidy problems with known answers. Open source hands you real
        code written by real developers. Nobody has to hire you first: fork a project, start
        hacking, and if your fix is good it gets merged straight back into the original. Along
        the way you learn:
      </p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>How large, real-world codebases are organised</li>
        <li>What a good commit and pull request look like</li>
        <li>How experienced maintainers review code, because they're reviewing yours</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="h3">It builds skills beyond code</h2>
      <p class="muted">
        Open source almost always means working with a team you've never met. That teaches you
        to:
      </p>
      <ul class="check-list">
        <li>Explain your changes clearly</li>
        <li>Take criticism without taking it personally</li>
        <li>Work through disagreements</li>
        <li>Split up work and decide what matters most</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="muted">In a crowded pool of developers, these are what make you stand out.</p>

      <h2 class="h3">Your work actually matters</h2>
      <p class="muted">
        Your contribution doesn't sit in a folder after it's graded. It ships, and people use
        it. You might fix a bug in software the world depends on, or help build the next big
        idea from its first commit.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h3">You'll find your people</h2>
      <p class="muted">
        Open source runs on something close to the African philosophy of Ubuntu:
      </p>
      <div class="note"><span>"I am what I am because of what we all are."</span></div>
      <p class="muted">
        People from different countries and backgrounds build things together that none of them
        could build alone, and the friendships made along the way often last for years. Come
        for the prospects, stay for the community.
      </p>

      <h2 class="h3">How to get started</h2>
      <div class="steps">
        <div class="step">
          <span class="step__n">1</span>
          <div>
            <div class="step__t">Learn the basics</div>
            <div class="step__d">
              Read <a class="link-arrow" href="/getting-started/101/">The Basics 101</a> to see what
              open source actually means and what contributing looks like.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <span class="step__n">2</span>
          <div>
            <div class="step__t">Practise your first pull request</div>
            <div class="step__d">
              Try the fork, branch, PR and merge flow on{' '}
              <a class="link-arrow" href={playground.repo} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{playground.name}</a>,
              a low-stakes sandbox made for exactly this.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <span class="step__n">3</span>
          <div>
            <div class="step__t">Pick a real issue</div>
            <div class="step__d">
              Look for issues labelled <code>good first issue</code> on a project you like.
              They're picked to be approachable for newcomers.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="step">
          <span class="step__n">4</span>
          <div>
            <div class="step__t">Ask for help</div>
            <div class="step__d">
              Stuck? Ask on the <a class="link-arrow" href={LINKS.slack} target="_blank" rel="noopener">PESOS Slack</a>.
              Someone will point you in the right direction.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>
  </GuideDoc>
</BaseLayout>
